The schema contains nine entity groupings: one persistent Aircraft identified by icao24; one time-specific Observation identified by icao24 plus time; a Location and AbsolutePoint for each observation; one Velocity for each observation; separate barometric and geometric Altitude entities because the two measurements can differ; and Callsign and Squawk AlternateIdentifier entities.

The Aircraft corresponds to bsm:Aircraft. Its icao24 value maps directly to bsm:sourceIdentifier rather than creating another AlternateIdentifier, because it is the aircraft’s primary stable source identifier. The Aircraft links to each Observation through bsm:hasObservation.

The Observation corresponds to bsm:Observation and carries time through bsm:observationDateTime. It links to the Location through bsm:hasGeographicLocation, to Velocity through bsm:hasVelocity, to both Altitude entities through bsm:hasAltitude, and to the callsign and squawk identifiers through bsm:hasAlternateIdentifier.

Location and AbsolutePoint are separate because the ontology models bsm:Location as having a bsm:Geometry, while latitude and longitude belong specifically to bsm:AbsolutePoint. Velocity contains speed and movement bearing. The heading column maps to bsm:absoluteBearingAngle rather than the near-synonym bsm:headingAngle: the decisive qualifier is that the source explicitly describes a track angle over ground, not aircraft nose orientation.

Callsign and squawk are modeled as AlternateIdentifier entities because the schema supplies two distinct named identifier schemes, satisfying the ontology’s standard-code/value structure. Constants identify those schemes.

The vertical-rate field is not an inclination angle and therefore has no suitable property. The onground boolean describes surface-versus-airborne state, but bsm:operatingModeCode specifically expects a code; because the column is silent about any coding scheme and differs in datatype and meaning, it remains unmapped.

All IRI transformations were checked against their referenced subjects: observation, location, point, velocity, both altitude, callsign identifier, and squawk identifier expressions are character-for-character identical. No unused transformation keys are present.

